# Target C — bounded-face phase mixing consumes crossing capacity

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only structural candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The bounded-face endpoint fan gives an exact coupling between stopping-face phase
mixing and the train-crossing graph. If two train populations are counted at the same
bounded face, their pairs cannot cross. Bounded-face endpoint simplicity ensures that
the same pair is not charged twice at two bounded faces.

The implication chain is

```text
bounded-face fan + no repeated bounded-face pair
  -> every mixed face incidence is a distinct noncrossing train pair
  -> mixed-face incidence is bounded by missing train crossings
  -> near-Mantel/phase-saturated routes force bounded faces to be phase-pure.
```

This is an exact interface currency. It does not by itself control the outer face or
close C-II.

## 1. Endpoint populations

Let `A` and `B` be disjoint families of complete stopped trains. They may be selected
by projective direction windows, phase atoms, or any other train property. For a
bounded nonquadrilateral face `F`, define

\[
a_F:=\#\{\tau\in A:\tau\text{ has a terminal occurrence on }F\},
\qquad
b_F:=\#\{\tau\in B:\tau\text{ has a terminal occurrence on }F\}.
\tag{1.1}
\]

The no-loop theorem implies that one train has at most one terminal occurrence on a
given bounded face. The bounded-face endpoint-simplicity theorem implies that two
distinct trains cannot have terminal occurrences on the same two bounded faces.

Let `X(A,B)` be the number of unordered pairs `(tau,sigma)` in `A x B` whose
canonical midpoint paths cross in a strict rhombus. Let `N(A,B)` be the remaining
pairs. Since `A` and `B` are disjoint,

\[
|A||B|=X(A,B)+N(A,B).
\tag{1.2}
\]

## 2. Mixed-face pairs are distinct missing crossings

Fix a bounded face `F`. Every pair consisting of one train in `A` incident with `F`
and one train in `B` incident with `F` shares that stopping face. The bounded-face fan
theorem says that the two paths cannot cross. Therefore each of the `a_Fb_F` pairs
is in `N(A,B)`.

If the same pair of trains were counted at two different bounded faces `F` and `G`,
both trains would join the same pair of bounded faces, contradicting bounded-face
endpoint simplicity. Thus the sets of pairs counted by distinct faces are disjoint.
Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{F\text{ bounded}}a_Fb_F\le N(A,B)=|A||B|-X(A,B).
}
\tag{2.1}
\]

This is finite and occurrence-safe. No face degree is replaced by a distinct-vertex
count, and no contact-isolated source point enters the argument.

## 3. Directional crossing form

Let `R(A,B)` be the set of strict-rhombus faces whose two crossing trains lie in `A`
and `B`, and put `A(A,B)=sum_{R in R(A,B)} area(R)`. Suppose every such crossing has
rhombus angle `alpha` in a set `I subset (pi/3,pi/2]`. The corresponding rhombus
areas are `sin alpha`, so the crossing count is bounded by the area carried by those
crossings:

\[
X(A,B)\ge {A(A,B)\over\sup_{\alpha\in I}\sin\alpha}
\quad\text{whenever all }(A,B)\text{ crossings lie in }I,
\tag{3.1}
\]

where `X(A,B)=|R(A,B)|`. More generally, if the crossing angles lie in the window
`pi/3<=alpha<=pi/3+eta` with `0<eta<pi/6`, then

\[
X(A,B)\ge {A(A,B)\over\sin(\pi/3+\eta)}.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Combining this with (2.1) gives the crossing-capacity inequality

\[
\boxed{
\sum_F a_Fb_F
\le |A||B|-X(A,B).
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

Thus an argument that makes `X(A,B)` close to the available bipartite capacity
`|A||B|` automatically makes the bounded-face phase product small.

## 4. Near-Mantel consequence

Along a sequence for which

\[
X(A,B)\ge |A||B|-o(D^2),
\tag{4.1}
\]

the exact inequality gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_F a_Fb_F=o(D^2).
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

In particular, no single bounded face can carry `Omega(D)` endpoints from both
families. More generally, if a family of bounded faces has
`a_F>=rho D` and `b_F>=sigma D` for each member, then that family has at most
`o(1) / (rho sigma)` members along the sequence.

If the two families are narrow phase windows separated by the rhombic crossing angle,
(4.2) is a phase-purity statement for every macroscopic bounded stopping face. It
also supplies a quantitative penalty whenever a proposed interface construction
places both crossing phase populations on the same exceptional face.

## 5. Scope and remaining gap

The inequality is useful only when an independent argument supplies a lower bound on
`X(A,B)` or an upper bound on the missing-crossing budget. The current C-II ledger
does not provide that bound uniformly: multi-grain phase populations and repeated
outer-face endpoints can absorb the missing crossings. The exact half-disk scalar
witness therefore remains feasible.

The surviving source-level target is to connect (2.1) to one of the current payment
currencies—`e_BB-k`, `P_exc`, or the aggregate outer detour—or to use the resulting
phase-pure corridor family in a source-preserving diameter descent.

No theorem-state file is changed. The result is proof-only.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting hostile audit; C-II remains open.
